  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 108 AM CST Fri Nov 6 2026\n\nLow clouds have been moving in over areas east of the Missouri River \nand over northeastern SD, with fog developing both under and outside \nof the low cloud coverage. With high surface pressure over central \nand northeastern SD through the afternoon causing the winds to be \nlight, this fog will be sticking around into the late morning/early \nafternoon. A dense fog advisory has been issued as the fog will be \ndense and visibilities could be less than a mile at times in these \nareas. Another concern with the fog is that the temperatures will be \ndropping below freezing in areas with the fog, creating freezing \nfog. Some model soundings keep the temperatures under the low clouds \nfrom dropping below freezing, while other models have the \ntemperatures drop down. Take caution when traveling this morning as \nthe lower visibilities from the fog and the potential for slick \nsurfaces from freezing fog could create slightly hazardous travel \nconditions through the morning. The fog and low clouds will help to \nkeep the temperatures from rising to much during the day, so they \nwill be around normal in the low 40s to low 50s. \n\nThe lower clouds and fog will be building back in this evening into \nSaturday morning over central and northeastern SD. The low \ntemperatures look to be below freezing again, which could create \nareas of freezing fog into Saturday morning. This fog is forecast to \nmix out during the morning and temperatures will be warming up. High \ntemperatures are forecast to warm 10 to 20 degrees warmer than \nnormal with these above normal temperatures continuing into the \nbeginning of the work week. Despite a few frontal boundaries and \nsurface troughs moving through central and northeastern SD this \nweekend, drier air aloft through the beginning of the work week will \nhelp to keep precipitation from developing.\n\nA small upper level shortwave will be moving to the north of SD \nSaturday into Sunday, which will help to move a low surface pressure \nover Canada to the east. The low surface pressure tightens the \npressure gradient over northern SD Saturday that will create some \nstronger winds aloft over northern SD Saturday. The model ensembles \nhave a 30-50% chance for winds to gust up to and over 30 mph early \nSaturday afternoon over north central SD and portions of \nnortheastern SD north of HWY 212. The models have backed off \nslightly on the winds, as they only have a 10-20% chance for gusts \nup to 35 mph over north central compared to the lower chances for up \nto 40 mph in earlier runs. Minimum relative humidity in the \nafternoon will be between 35-55% in these areas, which helps to keep \nthe fire weather concerns from increasing too much. The grassland \nfire danger index values for north central and northern portions of \nnortheastern SD will be mostly be moderate with portions of western \nCorson county getting into the high category.",
